\chapter{Main Result}
We show that the greedy algorithm retains a constant-factor
guarantee when the budget distribution satisfies a mild
regularity condition.

\begin{theorem}
If the budget distribution is log-concave, the greedy policy
achieves an expected approximation ratio of at least $1/2 - o(1)$.
\end{theorem}

The proof combines a martingale argument with a truncation bound;
technical lemmas are deferred to the appendix.

\section{Preliminary Lemmas}

\begin{lemma}
Let $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + \Theta(n)$ describe the running time of the
sampling subroutine. Then $T(n) = \Theta(n \log n.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The recursion tree has $\log_2 n$ levels, each contributing $\Theta(n)$
work, and the bound follows by summation.
\end{proof}

\section{Truncation Bound}
For any threshold $\tau$, truncating the budget at $\tau$ changes the
expected objective by at most the tail mass beyond $\tau$.
Log-concavity implies sub-exponential tails, which controls this term.

Combining the two lemmas with the martingale argument from the main
text completes the proof of the theorem.

